
    <section xml:id="Math2301Day12">
      <title>Linear Aggression</title>

      <subsection xml:id="review">
        <title>Review</title>
        <p>
          Recall the definitions from last time:
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>What properties does an inner product have?</li>
            <li>How do I go from an inner product to a norm?</li>
            <li>What does it mean for two vectors to be orthogonal?</li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="regressing">
        <title>Regressing</title>
        <p>
          A classic tool used throughout the sciences that you may already be familiar with is <term>linear regression</term>. For this you have a set of <m>m</m> points <m>(x_{1},y_{1}), \dots, (x_{m},y_{m})</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^{2}</m> and you want to find the <term>line of best fit</term> for those points.
        </p>
        <p>
          What does it mean to fit best? We want to minimize the error of our predictions. For example, suppose we get the line <m>y = \beta_{0} + \beta_{1} x</m>. Then the error at the point <m>(x_{i}, y_{i})</m> is
          <me>|y_{i} - (\beta_{0} + \beta_{1}x_{i})|</me>.
          When minimizing error over many points, there's a choice here. It turns out we can think of this from a geometric perspective. Namely, we can store our error terms in a vector:
          <me>\vec{r} = (y_{1} - \beta_{0} -\beta_{1}x_{1},y_{2} - \beta_{0} -\beta_{1}x_{2}, \dots, y_{m} -\beta_{0}-\beta_{1}x_{m})</me>.
          In other words,
          <me>r_{i} = y_{i} - \beta_{0} -\beta_{1} x_{i}</me>.
          The vector <m>\vec{r}</m> is called the <term>residual vector</term>. When we are minimizing error, we think of this geometrically as minimizing the distance from <m>\vec{r}</m> to the origin. From the distance equation, or really, the Pythagorean theorem,
          <me>\text{dist}(\vec{r},\vec{0}) = \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{m} r_{i}^{2}} = \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{m} (y_{i}-\beta_{0}-\beta_{1}x_{i})^{2}}</me>.
          The notation we use for this is called a norm, and it's like an absolute value with <m>2</m> bars:
          <me>\|\vec{r}\| = \text{dist}(\vec{r}, \vec{0}) = \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{m} r_{i}^{2}}</me>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Minimizing the square root of a non-negative function is the same as just minimizing the square. Thus, we want to solve the following optimization problem:
          <me>\min_{\beta_{0}, \beta_{1}} \sum_{i=1}^{m} (y_{i}-\beta_{0}-\beta_{1}x_{i})^{2}</me>.
          We can further rewrite this expression. Define the matrix:
          <me>X = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; x_{1} \\1 &amp; x_{2} \\ \vdots &amp; \vdots \\ 1 &amp; x_{m}\end{pmatrix}</me>,
          and define
          <me>\vec{y} = \begin{pmatrix} y_{1} \\ y_{2} \\ \vdots \\y_{m} \end{pmatrix} \text{ and } \vec{\beta} = \begin{pmatrix} \beta_{0}\\\beta_{1} \end{pmatrix}</me>.
          Then we can rewrite the residuals more compactly:
          <me>\vec{r} = \vec{y} - X \vec{\beta}</me>.
          Note that <m>\vec{y}</m> and <m>X</m> are fixed as part of our input data. The only thing that varies is <m>\vec{\beta}</m>. This gives rise to a general problem.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="best-approximations-for-solving-linear-systems">
        <title>Best Approximations for Solving Linear Systems</title>
        <p>
          We have discussed ad nauseam that a system of linear equations
          <me>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</me>
          need not have a solution. However, what should we do if there is no solution? Can we please just give up? No! Of course not.
        </p>
        <p>
          What we could instead ask is how close are we to having a solution. Concretely we have a solution if
          <me>A \vec{x} - \vec{b} = \vec{0}</me>.
          We could instead ask to minimize <m>\|A\vec{x}-\vec{b}\|</m>. To do this, we minimize the distance to the origin expressed as
          <me>\min_{\vec{x} \text{ in }\mathbb{R}^{n}} \|\vec{b} - A \vec{x}\|</me>.
          If we can solve this, we can do linear regression and find a line of best fit. Thus, this is our goal. We can reformulate this a bit further in a way that will be helpful. Namely, vectors of the form <m>A \vec{x}</m> are exactly the column span of <m>A</m>. In other words,
          <me>\min_{\vec{x} \text{ in }\mathbb{R}^{n}} \|\vec{b} - A\vec{x}\| = \min_{\vec{y} \text{ in } \text{ColSpan}(A)} \| \vec{b} - \vec{y}\|</me>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Thus, another way to view this problem is that we have a vector <m>\vec{b}</m> and a linear subspace <m>W</m>. How can we find the closest vector to <m>\vec{b}</m> in that subspace? Our next goal is to solve this problem, and for it, we will need to develop some theory.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="the-gram-schmidt-process">
        <title>The Gram-Schmidt Process</title>
        <p>
          Suppose I have a basis for a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^{n}</m>: <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{k}</m>. Let <m>\vec{u} \in \mathbb{R}^{n}</m>. Then if we're very lucky <m>\vec{u}</m> is in that subspace, but what if it is not? How do we find the distance to that subspace? It turns out there is a recipe for doing this called the <term>Gram-Schmidt Process</term>. The idea is as follows: Find a basis to write every vector in for which computing distances is easier.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{n}</m> be vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^{n}</m>. We call <m>\vec{v}_{1},\dots, \vec{v}_{n}</m> an <term>orthonormal basis</term> if
          <me>\vec{v}_{i} \cdot \vec{v}_{j} = \begin{cases} 1 &amp;\text{if }i=j\\0 &amp;\text{otherwise.} \end{cases}</me>
          The reason this is a basis is that if we let <m>A</m> be the matrix with columns <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{n}</m> and let <m>B = A^{\intercal} A</m>, then by definition of matrix multiplication, <m>B_{i,j} = \vec{v}_{i} \cdot \vec{v}_{j}</m>. Then
          <me>B_{i,j} = \vec{v}_{i} \cdot \vec{v}_{j} = \begin{cases} 1 &amp;\text{if }i=j\\0 &amp;\text{otherwise.} \end{cases}</me>
          Thus, <m>B</m> has ones along the diagonal and zeroes otherwise and is therefore an identity matrix. Thus, <m>A^{-1} = A^{\intercal}</m>. We can recall from our group theory unit that we call such a matrix <term>orthogonal</term>.
        </p>
        <theorem>
          <statement>
            <p>
              For any basis <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \vec{v}_{2},\dots, \vec{v}_{n}</m> of <m>\mathbb{R}^{n}</m>, there exists an orthonormal basis <m>\vec{u}_{1}, \dots, \vec{u}_{n}</m> such that
              <me>\text{span}(\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{k}) = \text{span}(\vec{u}_{1},\dots,\vec{u}_{k})</me>
              for all <m>1 \leq k \leq n</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <proof>
            <p>
              First, we let
              <me>\vec{u}_{1} = \frac{\vec{v}_{1}}{\sqrt{\vec{v}_{1} \cdot \vec{v}_{1}}}</me>.
              Then for <m>k \geq 2</m> we define
              <me>\vec{w}_{k} = \vec{v}_{k} - \sum_{i=1}^{k-1} (\vec{u}_{i}\cdot \vec{v}_{k} )\vec{u}_{i}</me>,
              and
              <me>\vec{u}_{k} = \frac{\vec{w}_{k}}{\sqrt{\vec{w}_{k} \cdot \vec{w}_{k}}}</me>.
              Then for <m>j \lt k</m>, assuming <m>\vec{u}_{1},\ldots,\vec{u}_{k-1}</m> are orthonormal,
              <md>
                <mrow>\vec{u}_{j} \cdot \vec{w}_{k} \amp = \vec{u}_{j} \cdot \left(\vec{v}_{k} -\sum_{i=1}^{k-1} (\vec{u}_{i}\cdot \vec{v}_{k} )\vec{u}_{i}\right)</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \vec{u}_{j} \cdot \vec{v}_{k} -\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}(\vec{u}_{i}\cdot \vec{v}_{k} )(\vec{u}_{j} \cdot \vec{u}_{i})</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = \vec{u}_{j} \cdot \vec{v}_{k} - (\vec{u}_{j} \cdot \vec{v}_{k})(\vec{u}_{j}\cdot \vec{u}_{j})</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = 0</mrow>
              </md>.
              Since <m>\vec{u}_{k}</m> is just a rescaling of <m>\vec{w}_{k}</m>, it follows that <m>\vec{u}_{j} \cdot \vec{u}_{k} = 0</m>. By construction, <m>\vec{u}_{k} \cdot \vec{u}_{k} = 1</m>. That the spans coincide follows by construction.
            </p>
          </proof>
        </theorem>
        <p>
          The Gram-Schmidt process is the algorithm used to make this happen:
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>Start with <m>\vec{v}_{1},\dots, \vec{v}_{n}</m>.</li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Iteratively replace <m>\vec{v}_{k}</m> with <m>\vec{u}_{k}=\frac{\vec{w}_{k}}{\sqrt{\vec{w}_{k} \cdot \vec{w}_{k}}}</m>, where
                <me>\vec{w}_{k} = \vec{v}_{k} - \sum_{i=1}^{k-1}(\vec{u}_{i}\cdot \vec{v}_{k})\vec{u}_{i}</me>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          Then <m>\vec{u}_{1},\dots,\vec{u}_{n}</m> is an orthonormal basis.
        </p>
        <p>
          The beauty of having an orthonormal basis is that distances are easy to compute. To start, as discussed above, the distance from a vector <m>\vec{a}</m> to the origin is given by the distance formula:
          <me>\|\vec{a}\| = \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i}^{2}} = \sqrt{\vec{a} \cdot \vec{a}}</me>.
          Now let's use an orthonormal basis <m>\vec{u}_{1},\dots,\vec{u}_{n}</m>. Then
          <me>\vec{a} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} b_{i} \vec{u}_{i}</me>,
          where <m>b_{i} = \vec{a} \cdot \vec{u}_{i}</m>. Then
          <md>
            <mrow>\|\vec{a}\|^{2} \amp = \vec{a} \cdot \vec{a}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = \left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}b_{i} \vec{u}_{i}\right) \cdot\left(\sum_{j=1}^{n} b_{j}\vec{u}_{j} \right)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = \sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n} b_{i}b_{j}(\vec{u}_{i} \cdot \vec{u}_{j})</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = \sum_{i=1}^{n} b_{i}^{2}(\vec{u}_{i}\cdot\vec{u}_{i})</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = \sum_{i=1}^{n} b_{i}^{2}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = \sum_{i=1}^{n} (\vec{u}_{i} \cdot \vec{a})^{2}</mrow>
          </md>.
          Thus,
          <me>\|\vec{a}\| = \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n} (\vec{u}_{i} \cdot \vec{a})^{2}} = \|C\vec{a}\|</me>,
          where <m>C</m> is the matrix with rows <m>\vec{u}_{1}, \dots, \vec{u}_{n}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          This actually allows us to solve the distance to a subspace minimization problem. Namely, the subspace is <m>\text{span}(\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{k})</m>.
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>Extend <m>\vec{v}_{1},\dots,\vec{v}_{k}</m> to a basis of all of <m>\mathbb{R}^{n}</m>, <m>\vec{v}_{1},\dots, \vec{v}_{n}</m>.</li>
            <li>Apply Gram-Schmidt to <m>\vec{v}_{1},\dots, \vec{v}_{n}</m> to get <m>\vec{u}_{1},\dots, \vec{u}_{n}</m>.</li>
            <li>Take the matrix <m>U</m> with rows <m>\vec{u}_{k+1}, \dots, \vec{u}_{n}</m>. Then <m>\|U \vec{b}\|</m> is the distance, and <m>\vec{b} - U^{\intercal} U\vec{b}</m> gives the closest vector in that subspace.</li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="exercises">
        <title>Exercises</title>
        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Draw pictures and compute the following:
              <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>What is the distance from <m>(3,4)</m> to <m>(0,0)</m>?</li>
                <li>What is the distance from <m>(1,4,5)</m> to the <m>x</m>-<m>y</m> plane?</li>
                <li>What is the distance from <m>(1,3,2)</m> to the <m>z</m>-axis?</li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p>Consider the points <m>(1,1), (2,4), (3,9), (4,16)</m>. Write down the system of equations for checking if there is a line through all of these points.</p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Let <m>\vec{a} =(a_{1}, a_{2},\dots,a_{n})</m> and <m>\vec{b} = (b_{1}, b_{2}, \dots, b_{n})</m> be in <m>\mathbb{R}^{n}</m>.
              <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Explain why the set of vectors orthogonal to <m>\vec{a}</m> are exactly the solutions to the equation
                    <me>\begin{pmatrix} a_{1} &amp; a_{2} &amp; \dots &amp; a_{n} \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}x_{1} \\x_{2}\\ \vdots \\x_{n} \end{pmatrix} = 0</me>.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>Find an analogous equation describing the set of vectors orthogonal to <m>\vec{b}</m>.</li>
                <li>Write a system of two equations describing the set of vectors orthogonal to both <m>\vec{a}</m> and <m>\vec{b}</m>.</li>
                <li>How would you generalize your answer to part (c) to an arbitrary number <m>k</m> of vectors?</li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Consider the point <m>(3,4)</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^{2}</m>.
              <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>Compute <m>(3,4)/\|(3,4)\|</m>.</li>
                <li>Find a vector orthogonal to <m>(3,4)</m>.</li>
                <li>Find an orthonormal basis for <m>\mathbb{R}^{2}</m> using your answers for parts (a) and (b).</li>
                <li>Find the closest point to <m>(10,10)</m> on the line <m>\text{span}((3,4))</m>.</li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Apply the Gram-Schmidt process to the following three vectors to find an orthonormal basis for <m>\mathbb{R}^{3}</m>:
              <me>\vec{v}_{1} = \begin{pmatrix} 1\\1\\0 \end{pmatrix}, \vec{v}_{2} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\0 \\1\end{pmatrix}, \vec{v}_{3} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\1\\1 \end{pmatrix}</me>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </subsection>
    </section>
